      "confidence": 0.85,
      "curator_note": "Lower (sigma) branch of the generic branching scheme R-CH2-Y(+.) -> R(+) + .CH2-Y. Same C1-C2 bond cleaves as in the alpha branch but the charge migrates to R and the odd electron stays on the Y-bearing fragment, so no new pi bond forms. Book draws 0 electron-pushing arc(s); the page shows only two labeled reaction arrows (alpha up, sigma down) off a brace, so both encoded moves are implicit fishhooks required for electron balance (not drawn on the page): move 1 = one C1-C2 sigma electron stays on C2 as the carbon radical, move 2 = the other C1-C2 sigma electron migrates onto Y(N3), pairing with its odd electron to restore a neutral N lone pair; C1 (R) departs with the charge as CH3(+). The arrow-checker soft-flag on atom 3 ('radical change -1 vs arrows +1, consistent with a lone pair rather than radicals') is expected and correct: an electron arriving at the singly-occupied N orbital quenches the radical to a lone pair. GENERIC scheme (R, Y): instantiated as R=CH3, Y=NH2 (ethylamine radical cation, N-ionized); all m/z are curator-computed for this instantiation, none are printed in the book."
